WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.480
Checking cases can be a complete proof, if there are

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.050
only finitely many and you check them all.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.080
For endlessly many cases, checking is never enough

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:09.810
on its own.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.120
But a check plus a rule that passes the result along can

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:13.990
cover them all.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.380
The danger with case checking is a missing case.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.640
A proof by cases must show that the cases cover

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.850
everything, with nothing left outside.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.700
You have plenty of three-cent and five-cent stamps.

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.100
Which totals can you make exactly?

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.260
Pause and test the totals from one to twelve.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:37.260
Three and five work.

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.000
Six is two threes.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.520
Eight is three and five.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:42.300
Nine is three threes.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.900
Ten is two fives.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:46.140
Eleven is five and two threes.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:47.960
Twelve is four threes.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.600
But one, two, four and seven cannot be made.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:53.600
Now the key step.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:56.320
Eight, nine and ten can all be made.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.420
Add a three-cent stamp to each and you get eleven,

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:00.580
twelve and thirteen.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.180
Add another and you get fourteen, fifteen and sixteen.

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:08.120
Three totals in a row, plus the ability to add three,

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.260
reach every total from eight onwards.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:13.840
Every total of eight or more is possible.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:15.680
Seven cannot be made.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.480
Two fives are too many.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.400
With one five we would need two more, and with no fives

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.140
we would need a multiple of three.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.080
Neither works.

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:27.700
So seven is the largest total that cannot be made.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.490
A finite check can prove a never.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.730
Does any square number end in seven?

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.800
The last digit of a square depends only on the last

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:36.930
digit of the number.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.950
Check the ten digits: their squares end in zero, one,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.220
four, nine, six, five, six, nine, four and one.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.050
Seven never appears.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.570
Ten checks cover every whole number.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.910
For finitely many cases, check them all and show nothing

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:54.720
is left out.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.050
For endlessly many, check a starting block and give a

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.940
rule that carries the result forward.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:03.340
Notebook challenge: with four-cent and five-cent stamps,

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.900
what is the largest total you cannot make?
